WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:01.705
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
A subscription

00:00:01.705 --> 00:00:03.870
can use either the Push
or a Pull mechanism

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.460
for message delivery.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.100
But which one makes sense
for your application?

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.864
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:09.864 --> 00:00:12.708
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.615 --> 00:00:18.220
Welcome to "Pub/Sub Made Easy."

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:20.340
I am Priyanka, and
in today's episode,

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:22.940
we will learn to pick the
right delivery mechanism

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:24.950
for our Pub/Sub messages.

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:28.010
In the last episode, we
learned that in Pull delivery,

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:31.520
Subscriber application initiates
the request to the Cloud

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:35.570
Pub/Sub to receive messages,
while in Push delivery,

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:38.420
Cloud Pub/Sub initiates the
request to the Subscriber

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:40.940
application to deliver messages.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.010
How do we choose
between Push and Pull

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:45.360
mechanism for our use cases?

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.480
Well, let's look at
this step by step

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.000
by comparing a few properties.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.450
What kind of endpoints are
needed for Push versus Pull?

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:57.230
Well, in Pull mechanism, because
the subscriber is pulling

00:00:57.230 --> 00:01:00.920
messages from Cloud
Pub/Sub via the API,

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:04.040
any device on the internet
that has authorized credentials

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:07.340
to call the API can
serve as a Pull endpoint.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:09.390
In case of Push
mechanism, the message

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:13.220
is being pushed by Cloud Pub/Sub
to the subscriber endpoint,

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.280
so the endpoint must be
reachable via DNS name,

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.830
and have SSL
certificate installed.

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:22.080
It should be a
non-self-signed certificate.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:23.810
The benefit of
Push subscriptions

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:27.410
is that they are largely
decoupled from the Pub/Sub.

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:30.650
They don't need to have
credentials or use the Pub/Sub

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:32.090
client library.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:34.280
They just need a bit
of application logic

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:37.970
that knows how to parse
Pub/Sub host request body.

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:40.940
It's a very lightweight
integration model.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:44.510
How do you balance load
while using Push versus Pull?

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:47.870
Well, in Pull mechanism,
multiple subscribers

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:51.560
can make Pull calls to the
same shared subscription.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:55.190
Each subscriber will receive
a subset of the messages.

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:57.500
While in Push
mechanism, the Push

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:01.100
endpoint itself can be a
load balancer distributing

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:03.530
the request to the
right back end.

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:06.710
The real use case for this
Push model is front end,

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:10.160
multiple subscriptions
pointed to the same endpoint.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.180
So as you add new topics, you
don't have to change anything

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:15.770
about your subscriber.

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:17.630
How do you control
the flow of messages

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:19.460
received by the subscriber?

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:22.070
What if the subscriber cannot
handle the messages fast

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:22.940
enough?

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:25.640
Well, in Pull mechanism,
the subscriber client

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:28.370
controls the rate of
delivery-- because, you know,

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:30.470
it's the one pulling
those messages.

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:32.900
The subscriber can
dynamically modify

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:36.050
the acknowledgment deadline,
allowing message processing

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:38.500
to be arbitrarily long.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:41.360
While in Push scenarios,
the Cloud Pub/Sub server

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:43.760
automatically implements
the flow control

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:45.770
by backing off
exponentially whenever

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:50.030
the endpoint returns error, or
takes a long time to respond.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:52.280
There's no need to handle
message flows at the client

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:53.180
side.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:55.790
Although the client can
indicate that it cannot handle

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:59.720
the current message load by
passing back an HTTP error.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:01.800
When it comes to
efficiency and throughput,

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.770
Pull mechanism achieves
high throughput

00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:06.620
per CPU and bandwidth by
allowing batch delivery

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:08.360
processing and acknowledgments.

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:10.070
The downside is
that you must always

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:12.650
have an application running
to keep getting messages

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:14.330
from Pub/Sub.

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:17.360
With Push delivery, you can
take advantage of the scale

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:20.600
to zero serverless pattern
supported by Cloud Functions,

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:22.460
Cloud Run, or App Engine.

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:26.750
This is useful when your request
rate is low or very uneven.

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:30.320
The Push mechanism delivers
one message per request,

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:33.770
and limits the maximum number
of outstanding messages.

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:36.170
Now, to the most
important question--

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:39.900
which mechanism is best
for your application?

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:40.850
Well, here goes.

00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:43.490
You would typically
set up Pull subscribers

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:46.370
if the volume of
messages is large--

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:49.320
like, many more
than 1 per second.

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:53.580
If efficiency and throughput of
message processing is critical,

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:58.070
and if public HTTPS endpoint
with non-self-signed SSL

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:00.950
certificate is not
feasible to setup.

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:03.830
And it's recommended
to use Push subscribers

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:06.710
if there are multiple topics
that must be processed

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:10.340
by the same webhook, with
App Engine standard and Cloud

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:13.730
Function subscribers, and if
you use an environment where

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:15.780
Google Cloud Platform
dependencies,

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:18.050
such as credentials
and client libraries,

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:20.180
are not feasible to set up.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:21.920
All right, so today
we learned how

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:25.190
to choose between Push and
Pull subscription models.

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:28.370
We compared the set for
endpoint, load balancing,

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:32.480
efficiency, and flow control
for Push and Pull subscribers.

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:34.340
These are factors
that play a role

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:37.280
in finally deciding
which model to choose

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:39.080
in your specific scenario.

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:42.590
Join us next time to learn more
about setting up the subscriber

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:45.890
to receive messages
using Pull delivery.

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:48.960
If you like this video and would
